x86_64-linux  one recorded rule. Every constructor here is inline, so clang writes only the form a use asks for and cxx1 writes both of Itanium's - docs/CONFORMANCE.md, "An inline member function is a strong symbol" - which is the six C1 forms cxx1 has and clang does not. The cleanup clang emits for `new M` and `new P(1)` - _Unwind_Resume, the personality, _ZdlPv, freeing the storage if the constructor throws - cxx1 emits too since 2026-09-26 ([expr.new]/20), so those three names agree now. The Windows comparison is where the clone of a `++n` operand used to spell the C name. It does not agree exactly - an earlier version of this line said it did, from clang's Microsoft output rather than from cl - see this case's .nocl
arm64-darwin  the same rule: Base's C1 is cxx1's extra, and the new-cleanup names agree.
x86_64-windows  cxx1's cleanup for `new M` is a funclet that calls ??3@YAXPEAX@Z, and the scrape reads that call from cxx1's funclet; clang's cleanup lives in a ?dtor$ funclet whose name the scrape drops on both sides and whose calls it does not read. Nothing clang wrote is missing from cxx1's list.
